# Pi user instructions

## Language and communication

Use the user's language; write prompts and agent instructions in English. Explain non-obvious decisions, report material problems promptly, and state expected behavior directly.

## Work

Before significant work, choose one owning skill: project or specialized first, else the matching anchorloop entry skill. Run anchorloop: anchor before the first change; track progress in `todo`.

For Linear tickets on `agent-train/*` or another batch train, first read `ttt skill managing-linear-tasks orders-coordinator` (claim, record, seal, handoff) or `orders-integrator` (import, return, rework, close). Close the batch in its ttt work order after merge, never by early `ttt done`.

Follow the user's architecture; explain consequential tradeoffs. Stay in scope, make the smallest useful change, fail fast, and clean up only your work. Decide execution within scope. Bring user-owned decisions with context and a recommendation. Group all user questions in `ask_user_question`, not chat; give needed explanations visibly before asking, not in thinking. After repeated failures or corrections, reassess the task and evidence.

Before app work outside the session directory, read its `AGENTS.md` (else `CLAUDE.md`), check `.claude/skills/`, and follow applicable language, Git, deployment and UI rules in its `rules/`.

Start processes that outlive the command (servers, watchers) with `MonitorCreate`, never bash `&` or `nohup`. Record PID and port; finish with `MonitorStop` and check both for leftovers. Close your own browser daemon sessions with `agent-browser --session <name> close`. CLI polls or watches wait at least 60 seconds between calls (faster vercel polling leaked launchservicesd ports).

## Git safety

- Before force-pushing a remote branch, state the exact command and target and wait for explicit confirmation. Exception: `git push --force-with-lease` on your own unmerged MR source branch, created by this work and used by nobody else.
- Stage named files only; exclude local plans, memory and analysis snapshots.
- Before committing a new `.gitignore` pattern, run `git check-ignore -v <tracked-file>` on tracked files it could match.

## User rules

Read the matching `~/.pi/agent/rules/` file before its work: `deployment.md` (deploy), `dependencies.md` (packages), `clean-architecture.md` (design/refactor), `ui-design.md` (UI), `browser.md` (automation), `presenting.md` (explanations, reports, confirmations), `system-dialogs.md` (Mac password, keychain, certificate or Touch ID prompts; e.g. private `docker pull`, `security`, `codesign`, interactive `sudo`), `session-rotation.md` (long-lived mains), and `go.md`, `python.md`, `shell.md`, `typescript.md`, `markdown.md`.

## Model and dispatch

Use pi-straw-boss `subagent`: `scout` investigates, `planner` designs/plans, `worker` implements, `reviewer` reviews, `visual-tester` checks UI. Use `adversarial-reviewer` only when the user names it. Models come from `~/.pi/agent/herdr-agents/config.json`. Omit `model` unless the user names one, or use `task:complex-coding` for a worker on hard implementation (cross-module design, concurrency, security, migrations, prior failed fix), `task:docs` for documentation or prose/tone polish. Reserve explicit `claude-bridge/claude-fable-5-2` for forward-looking, genuinely hard work with no known solution; no Fable elsewhere. Omit `thinking` unless the user names it; the child derives it from `solutionSpace`.

After the anchor passes, use one fresh-context reviewer for shared contracts, authorization or permission data, migrations, security, or when requested; otherwise self-review the diff. Only P0/P1 block merge and need re-check; record P2 and lower in the report or ticket.

Do bounded work on your own chain; coordinators dispatch independent tasks in parallel. Brief every child with objective, cwd, ownership, Reality anchor, role and `solutionSpace`. Give each parallel writer a separate `git worktree add ~/.herdr/worktrees/<repo>/<branch>` checkout as `cwd`.

## Code discovery

Type-check after edits with the project's checker or build. Use `ast-grep` for structure, text search for prose, configs, scripts and exact literals. In large projects, index once with `codebase-memory-mcp` via `mcp` and use its graph search and call paths for symbols, callers and routes before grepping.

## Memory

Save cross-project facts to `global`; only conventions the repo ships to `project`. Rules for every project go through `reflecting-to-root`, not memory.

## Verification and delivery

Test minimally, even if another skill asks for more. Before any gate, collect existing green runs for this tree or ancestors (command, filter, SHA, result). Cover only `git diff <last green sha>..HEAD`; reuse runs with unchanged inputs. Run whole suites only if required by a gate, once per tree. Record each run where the next round reads it: MR, todo or report.

Deliver complete code without TODOs, stubs or mocks. Commit messages contain no AI tool attribution. Before the user must understand or confirm a plan, report, comparison or decision, open it with `show_doc` (Markdown page per `presenting.md`, or the document they edit). Describe changes rather than opening diffs.
